From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] crypt32: CNG algorithm names of built-in OIDs (1532)

CryptFindOIDInfo's built-in hash, encryption, public key and signature OIDs
carry, on Windows, the name CNG knows the algorithm by (pwszCNGAlgid: "SHA1",
"SHA256", "MD5", "3DES", "RSA", ...) and, for a signature, its public key
algorithm in pwszCNGExtraAlgid ("" where there is none). Wine left both NULL
except for ECDSA/ECC. Microsoft Access looks up the hash OID as it checks an
opened database's signature and reads its CNG name: opening any existing
.accdb crashed (wcslen of NULL), and Access then asked whether to open a
database that "caused a serious error".

Gate: test/oidcng-gate.sh.
Mutant: SG_MUTANT_OID_NO_CNG_ALGID (gate fails: (null)|(null)).
crypt32 oid conformance: 175 tests, no failures, as before.

--- a/dlls/crypt32/oid.c
+++ b/dlls/crypt32/oid.c
@@ -1230,6 +1230,16 @@
 static const CRYPT_DATA_BLOB domainCompTypesBlob = { sizeof(domainCompTypes),
  (LPBYTE)domainCompTypes };
 
+/* Stained Glass OS (wine-sg 1532): the CNG names Windows gives the hash,
+ * encryption, public key and signature OIDs (pwszCNGAlgid, and for a
+ * signature the public key's in pwszCNGExtraAlgid). Access reads the hash's
+ * as it checks a database's signature and faulted on NULL. */
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_OID_NO_CNG_ALGID
+#define CNG(name) NULL
+#else
+#define CNG(name) name
+#endif
+
 static const struct OIDInfoConstructor {
     DWORD   dwGroupId;
     LPCSTR  pszOID;
